## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping contiguous subarrays with a per-subarray cost. It forces candidates to choose between extending a current subarray and starting a new one, while also testing reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **DP state separation**: Maintaining 'best' (completed subarrays) vs 'active' (subarray currently being built)
- **Non-overlapping interval selection** with a fixed count constraint
- **Reconstruction** of the chosen subarrays from DP tables
- **Space optimization** using rolling arrays (2D → 1D)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const active = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));

  best[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], active[i-1][t]);

      if (t > 0) {
        active[i][t] = Math.max(
          active[i-1][t] + A[i-1],
          best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c
        );
      }
    }
  }

  return best[n][k];
}
```

**Time Complexity**: O(n × k)
**Space Complexity**: O(n × k), or O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a partition DP where segment values depend on a non-trivial per-substring metric (distinct counts). It extends simple sum-based DP to a more nuanced local score.

## Key Concepts
- DP over i and t to build up k segments
- Precompute distinctCount(l,r) for all substrings
- Backpointers to recover exact endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDistinctSegments(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cnt = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let L = 0; L < n; L++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let d = 0;
    for (let R = L; R < n; R++) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(R) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) d++;
      cnt[L][R] = d;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + cnt[p][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct endpoints from par
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce memory to O(nk) while keeping reconstruction?
- How adapt to larger alphabets or different segment metrics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a DP with two choices per step (1- or 2-length blocks), plus reconstruction and complexity awareness.

## Key Concepts

- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Transitions for 1-length and 2-length blocks
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      // 1-length block ending at i-1
      if(dp[i-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 1; }
      }
      // 2-length block ending at i-1
      if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-2] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 2; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back from (n, k)
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

